# Root-cause finding (2026-07-30) while attempting to fix "gap 10"
# (cfg_arch_global_variants.spl): that fixture's wrong value turned out NOT
# to be caused by missing/broken @cfg(<arch>) stripping (the stripping
# mechanism already runs correctly in run_file_jit's shared
# load_module_with_imports path, module_loader.rs:1655, confirmed by
# hand-stripping the source and reproducing the identical wrong value with
# NO @cfg present at all). The real, more general defect: a module-level
# `val`/`var`'s value is stored raw/untagged and is never tag-boxed before
# flowing into print()/string interpolation/formatting under JIT -- even
# with NO cross-module import at all (this generalizes gap 9, whose
# "cross-module" framing was too narrow: gap 9's own same-file function-call
# case worked only because a function RETURN re-tags the value; a direct
# reference, same-file or cross-file, does not). Copying to a local var
# first does not help -- the corruption travels with the raw bit pattern,
# not the read site. SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT does not catch it. See
# doc/08_tracking/bug/jit_run_file_pipeline_gaps_2026-07-30.md section 9,
# which additionally unifies this same defect with gap 8 (module-level val
# from a function-call initializer reads 0) -- gap 8's raw value is 0
# (write-side: the initializer never runs) instead of this fixture's 100,
# but the read-side misinterpretation is bit-for-bit the same mechanism.
# Expected (interpreter, correct): X=100
val X = 100

fn main():
    print("X={X}")
